    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ordu-baliq"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ordu-baliq.jpg" alt="Ordu-Baliq" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ordu-baliq">Ordu-Baliq</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ordu-baliq.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">James A. Glazier, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the mid-5th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> were a tribe of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiele">Tiele</a> under the Turkic Khaganate. They fought alongside the Tang against the Tibetan Empire and the Turks in 627, and after the Tang defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-turkic-khaganate">Western Turkic Khaganate</a> in 657 they defected to the Tang. In 742 the Uyghurs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karluks">Karluks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basmyls">Basmyls</a> rebelled against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-turkic-khaganate">Second Turkic Khaganate</a>. In 744 the Basmyls captured the Turkic capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otuken">Ötüken</a> and killed the reigning Özmiş <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">Khagan</a>; later that year a Uyghur–Karluk alliance defeated the Basmyls, killed their khagan, and the Basmyls ceased to exist as a people. Hostility between Uyghurs and Karluks then drove the Karluks west into Zhetysu. In 745 the Uyghurs killed Kulun Beg, the last khagan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Göktürks</a>, and sent his head to the Tang.</p><p>The new khagan, Kutlug I Bilge Khagan, claimed to be the supreme ruler of all the tribes and built his capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordu-baliq">Ordu-Baliq</a>. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-book-of-tang">New Book of Tang</a> his territory reached the lands of the Shiwei in the east and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai Mountains</a> in the west and controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobi-desert">Gobi Desert</a>, covering the whole former territory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>. The khaganate called itself the Toquz Oghuz, the 'Nine Tribes', known to the Tang as the Jiuxing: a tribal confederation under the Uyghur nobility. When Kutlug died in 747 his youngest son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayanchur-khan">Bayanchur Khan</a> succeeded him and, with profits from trading outposts built with the Tang, finished Ordu-Baliq and built Bai Baliq further up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selenga-river">Selenga River</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/silk"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/silk.jpg" alt="Silk" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/silk">Silk</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silk_raw_01a.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kuebi = Armin Kübelbeck, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The khaganate's wealth came from the steppe herds on which its economy was based, from the Tang, and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>. The Tang paid for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> help against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan">An Lushan</a> with 20,000 rolls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and honorary titles, and fixed the horse trade at 40 rolls of silk for every horse; in 762 they paid 100,000 pieces of silk to make the Uyghurs leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, in 780 Tun Baga Tarkhan demanded 1,800,000 strings of cash in gold and silk as compensation for Uyghurs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sogdians">Sogdians</a> killed leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, and in 822 the Tang paid 70,000 pieces of silk to send home troops whose help they had declined.</p><p>To control trade along the Silk Road the Uyghurs allied with the Sogdian merchants who controlled oases of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>; the Sogdians enabled them to trade in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-regions">Western Regions</a> and exchange Chinese silk for other goods, and their trading communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> gained Uyghur protection in return. Unlike the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, who could own only what they could carry, the Uyghurs built a fixed capital with Persian influences: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordu-baliq">Ordu-Baliq</a> was a fully fortified commercial centre with concentric walls, lookout towers, stables, stores, administrative buildings, quarters for trade and handicrafts, and palaces and temples, including a monastery, at its heart. The city stored the goods of the China trade and let the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a> receive traders at a stable court, but the vulnerability of a fixed city, as Thomas Barfield observed, was also to be the Uyghurs' downfall.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/an-lushan.jpg" alt="An Lushan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan">An Lushan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B8%9D%E9%89%B4%E5%9B%BE%E8%AF%B4.Recueil_Historique_des_Principaux_Traits_de_la_Vie_des_Empereurs_Chinois.95%E5%B8%A7%E5%9B%BE.%E5%BD%A9%E7%BB%98%E5%86%8C%E9%A1%B5.%E7%BA%A618%E4%B8%96%E7%BA%AA_(page_19_%E5%AE%A0%E5%B9%B8%E7%95%AA%E5%B0%86)_(%E5%AE%89%E7%A6%84%E5%B1%B1)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">书格, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> power rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">mounted archers</a> who could be hired and feared. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayanchur-khan">Bayanchur Khan</a> the khaganate brought the Sekiz Oghuz, Kyrgyz, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karluks">Karluks</a>, Türgesh, Toquz <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> and Chiks under its rule, and after the Tang defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-talas">Battle of Talas</a> in 751 the Uyghurs emerged as the dominant power of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan">An Lushan</a> rebelled, Emperor Suzong of Tang turned to Bayanchur in 756; in 757 about 4,000 Uyghur horsemen helped retake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, looting Luoyang for three days. In 758 Bayanchur defeated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yenisei-kyrgyz">Yenisei Kyrgyz</a> army and executed their khan. In 762 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogu-qaghan">Bögü Qaghan</a>, who had planned to invade the Tang, switched sides after negotiations, helped defeat the rebels at Luoyang and sacked the city.</p><p>The later wars were fought against the Tibetan Empire and the Karluks. In 790 a Uyghur and Tang force was beaten by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> at Tingzhou (Beshbalik), but in 791 the Uyghurs drove the Tibetans from Lingzhou and defeated them and the Karluks at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beshbaliq">Beiting</a>, sending the captured general Zan Rgyal Sum to Emperor Dezong. In 792 Baoyi Qaghan took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaochang">Gaochang</a> and destroyed a Tibetan force besieging Yushu; in 808 the Uyghurs seized Liang Province from the Tibetans, though a Tibetan raid of 816 came within two days of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordu-baliq">Ordu-Baliq</a>. Chongde repulsed another invasion in 821. The end came from the north: in 840 some 80,000 Yenisei Kyrgyz horsemen razed Ordu-Baliq, and in 843 a Tang army under Shi Xiong killed 10,000 Uyghurs at 'Kill the Barbarians' Mountain.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/manichaeism"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manichaeism.jpg" alt="Manichaeism" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/manichaeism">Manichaeism</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manicheans.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a> ruled a confederation of tribes under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> nobility from his court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordu-baliq">Ordu-Baliq</a>, where the policies of the empire were decided. The state borrowed from sedentary neighbours, with clear Persian influences in government, and its rulers took Chinese titles and brides: Bayanchur married Princess Ningguo, daughter of Emperor Suzong, while his daughter Princess Pijia married the Tang prince Li Chengcai, sealing the alliance against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan">An Lushan</a> in 756. Succession ran from father to son but was repeatedly violent: Tun Baga Tarkhan killed his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogu-qaghan">Bögü Qaghan</a> in 779 and imposed new laws designed to secure the khaganate's unity.</p><p>The ruling Yaghlakar dynasty ended in 795 when Qutluq Bilge Qaghan died and the general Qutluq II declared himself qaghan, founding the Ädiz dynasty. Zhaoli Qaghan was murdered in 832, and in 839 Zhangxin Qaghan was forced to commit suicide by the minister Qasar, who seized the throne with 20,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatuo">Shatuo</a> horsemen from Ordos. Nine ministers stood near the throne; one of them, Kulug Bagha, called in the Kyrgyz. Religion was a matter of state. Bögü Qaghan's conversion in 762 made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manichaeism">Manichaeism</a> the official faith, chosen over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> perhaps to show independence from the Tang; it was suppressed under Tun Baga Tarkhan but restored by his successors, and an inscription at Ordu-Baliq records that Manichaeans tried to turn people from their old shamanistic beliefs, a conversion from above that met resistance below.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gaochang"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gaochang.jpg" alt="Gaochang" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gaochang">Gaochang</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turpan-gaochang-d10.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Colegota, CC BY-SA 2.5 es</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The khaganate decided the fate of the Tang. Its horsemen restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> to the dynasty in 757 and 762, and for eighty years it sold horses, took tribute and married princesses on terms the Tang could not refuse. Its Sogdian alliance kept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> open between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-regions">Western Regions</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sogdians">Sogdians</a> were the main traders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">Silk</a> Roads, and a tax list from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaochang">Gaochang</a> kingdom of the 620s shows them in 29 of 35 recorded caravan transactions.</p><p>Its religious choice left a long trace. The khaganate carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manichaeism">Manichaeism</a> into China, and the Tang persecution that followed its collapse, with the edict of 843 ordering the Manichaean priests of the empire killed and Emperor Wuzong's wider ban on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, ended that toleration. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> who fled south founded the Ganzhou Uyghur Kingdom in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> and the Kingdom of Qocho near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turpan</a>, where they turned to Buddhism and, in Mahmud al-Kashgari's words, were 'the strongest of the infidels'; Kashgari praised their Turkic as 'pure' and 'most correct'. Qocho became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yelu-dashi">Yelü Dashi</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qara-khitai">Qara Khitai</a> in 1134 and in 1209 its ruler, the Idiqut Barchuk Art Tegin, submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, after which Uyghurs served as civil servants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>, which adopted the Old Uyghur alphabet as its official script. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karluks">Karluks</a>, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chigils">Chigils</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaghma">Yagmas</a>, later founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-khanid-khanate">Kara-Khanid Khanate</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/khitans"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khitans.jpg" alt="Khitans" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/khitans">Khitans</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MongolHuntersSong.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hu Gui, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The last decades were a cycle of Tibetan wars and palace killings. Chongde, considered the last great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a>, died in 824; his brother Zhaoli was murdered in 832, and in 839 Chongde's son Zhangxin was forced to kill himself by the minister Qasar, who seized the throne with 20,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatuo">Shatuo</a> horsemen. The same year brought famine, an epidemic and a winter so severe that it killed much of the livestock. In 840 Kulug Bagha, Qasar's rival, fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yenisei-kyrgyz">Yenisei Kyrgyz</a> and invited them to invade. With some 80,000 horsemen they razed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordu-baliq">Ordu-Baliq</a>, beheaded Qasar and burned the other cities of the empire.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> fled in two groups: 30,000 under the aristocrat Ormïzt and 100,000 under the new khagan Wujie, son of Baoyi, both seeking refuge in Tang territory. Emperor Wuzong closed the border, granted Ormïzt asylum in return for the use of his troops, and refused Wujie's demands for a city, food and protection for Manichaeans. Wujie invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a> in 841; on 13 February 843 a Tang army under Shi Xiong killed 10,000 Uyghurs at 'Kill the Barbarians' Mountain. Ormïzt's troops, refusing to be dispersed, were massacred by the general Liu Mian. Wujie was killed in 846 and his brother Enian Qaghan defeated in 847. The Kyrgyz took little interest in the empire they had destroyed, leaving Kulug Bagha in charge of the Orkhon Valley, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khitans">Khitans</a> seized in 890; other Uyghurs went south to Ganzhou and Qocho, or to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karluks">Karluks</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Uyghur Khaganate (744–840) succeeded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Göktürks</a> on the Mongolian steppe, rescued the Tang from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan">An Lushan</a>'s rebellion at a heavy price in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, and built a walled capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordu-baliq">Ordu-Baliq</a> to hold the profits of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> trade it ran with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sogdians">Sogdians</a>. Famine and a Kyrgyz invasion destroyed it, scattering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turpan</a>.</em></p>

